---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Rental Car Accident or Breakdown in Portugal | TripAxle"
  description="A collision or breakdown in a Portuguese hire car: making the scene safe, calling 112, the legal duties, the evidence, and telling the rental company."
  eyebrow="Practical Guides"
  summary="What to do, in order, when something goes wrong in a hire car in Portugal — with the legal duties separated clearly from the things your rental contract requires."
  updated="2026-09-03"
  published="2026-09-03"
  verified="2026-09-03"
  image={images.autumnForest}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  related={related}
  sources={cite("codigoDaEstrada", "eu112", "ansr")}
  sourcesNote="The legal duties below are taken from Articles 87 to 89 of the Código da Estrada as published by ANSR, and the emergency-number position from the European Commission. What your rental company requires of you is contractual and is in your own rental agreement — this guide tells you where to look, not what yours says."
  trustNote="Nothing here is legal advice, and no claim outcome is promised. Where the road code sets a duty it is quoted as such; everything else is practical sequencing."
>
  <QuickAnswer slot="answer">
    <p>
      Stop, put on the reflective vest before leaving the car, and place the
      warning triangle at least 30 metres behind it. Call <strong>112</strong> if
      anyone is hurt or the road is blocked — it is free from any phone. Exchange
      identification, vehicle owner, insurer and policy number with the other
      driver, as the road code requires. Photograph everything. Then telephone
      the rental company on the number in the agreement, before the car is moved
      or recovered.
    </p>
  </QuickAnswer>

  <AtAGlance items={glance} />

  <h2 id="safe">First: make the scene safe</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Stop, switch on the hazard lights, and leave them on.</li>
    <li>
      Put on the reflective vest <em>before</em> you open the door. Article 88
      requires the vest to be worn by whoever places the warning triangle,
      repairs the vehicle or removes it — and it is the reason the vest is
      normally stored in the cabin rather than the boot.
    </li>
    <li>
      Get everyone out on the side away from traffic and behind the barrier if
      there is one. Article 87 says people not involved in removing or repairing
      the vehicle should not remain on the carriageway.
    </li>
    <li>
      Place the warning triangle perpendicular to the road, at least 30 metres
      behind the vehicle and visible from at least 100 metres, taking extra care
      where visibility is reduced.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the car is driveable and the situation allows, Article 87 expects you
      to park it properly or move it clear of the carriageway and arrange its
      prompt removal.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="who-to-call">Who to call</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>112</strong> for police, ambulance or fire. The European
      Commission confirms 112 is available everywhere in the EU, free of
      charge, from fixed and mobile phones.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The rental company's assistance line.</strong> It is printed on
      the rental agreement and usually on a sticker in the car. Call it before
      arranging any recovery of your own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your excess-reimbursement insurer</strong>, if you bought a
      third-party policy — most require notification within a stated window.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="law">What the law requires of you</h2>
  <p>
    Article 89 of the Código da Estrada requires a driver involved in a
    collision to give the other parties their own identification, that of the
    vehicle's owner, and that of the insurer together with the policy number,
    producing the supporting documents when asked. In a rental car the owner is
    the rental company and the insurer details are on the rental documentation —
    which is a good reason to keep the agreement in the car rather than in a
    hotel room.
  </p>
  <p>
    Article 89 also requires that, if the collision results in death or injury,
    the driver waits at the scene for the arrival of an officer. Do not leave.
  </p>
  <p>
    Article 88 requires every car in circulation to carry an approved warning
    triangle and reflective vest, and Article 87 governs how a vehicle
    immobilised by breakdown or collision must be dealt with. All three carry
    fines, with higher ranges on motorways.
  </p>
  <Callout variant="warning" title="Check the triangle and vest exist at pickup">
    They are the driver's responsibility, not the rental company's, once you
    have the car. Confirm both are in the vehicle before you leave the lot and
    note where they are — the vest belongs somewhere you can reach without
    getting out.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="evidence">Evidence to collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Wide shots showing both vehicles, their positions and the road layout.</li>
    <li>Close shots of every point of contact on both vehicles.</li>
    <li>Both registration plates, in a frame that also shows the damage.</li>
    <li>Road signs, markings and traffic signals relevant to what happened.</li>
    <li>The other driver's licence and insurance document.</li>
    <li>Names and contact details of any independent witness.</li>
    <li>The time, and a note of weather and light conditions.</li>
    <li>If police attend, the officers' unit and any report reference.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The pickup photographs you took at collection do the other half of this job:
    they establish what the car looked like before.
    <a href="/what-to-photograph-rental-pickup/">The pickup photo checklist</a>
    explains why that matters more than most travelers expect.
  </p>

  <h2 id="statement">The European Accident Statement</h2>
  <p>
    Many European insurers use a standard two-part accident statement — the
    <em>declaração amigável</em> — completed jointly at the scene, with each
    driver keeping one copy. If a form is in the car's documentation, use it: it
    records the same facts in the same order in both languages.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read what you sign. Signing a description of events you do not agree with is
    difficult to undo afterwards, and there is no obligation to agree on fault
    at the roadside. If you cannot agree, record each version and leave it to
    the insurers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="supplier">Telling the rental company</h2>
  <p>
    Rental agreements almost universally require prompt notification of any
    incident, and frequently require that you do not authorise repairs or
    recovery yourself. Failing to notify, or arranging your own tow, is one of
    the common ways damage cover is lost — the cover is a contractual promise,
    and the contract sets conditions on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ask, and write down: the incident reference, whether the car may be driven,
    whether they are sending recovery, whether a replacement vehicle is
    provided, and where the car should end up.
    <a href="/portugal-car-rental-insurance/">How the cover works</a> explains
    what the waiver does and does not do once the claim starts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="assistance">Roadside assistance and replacement cars</h2>
  <p>
    Whether roadside assistance is included, and whether a replacement car is
    provided and how quickly, are terms of your rental — not rights. They
    commonly differ between a mechanical breakdown, which is the company's
    problem, and damage caused by the driver, which may not be. In remote parts
    of the interior, "how quickly" can mean hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are offered a replacement vehicle, inspect and photograph it exactly
    as you would at a normal pickup. It is a new car on your agreement and its
    existing damage is now your exposure.
  </p>

  <h2 id="breakdown">Breakdown without a collision</h2>
  <p>
    The safety sequence is identical: hazards, vest, everyone out and behind
    the barrier, triangle at 30 metres, then call. Article 87 also prohibits
    repairing a vehicle on the public road except where it is essential to
    removing it or, for an easily fixed fault, to continuing the journey — so
    changing a wheel on a motorway hard shoulder is not the plan. Get it
    recovered.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not leave the car unattended with luggage in it while you go for help. If
    you must leave, take the documents and anything valuable.
  </p>

  <h2 id="claim">Documents for the claim</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>The rental agreement and the incident reference the company gave you.</li>
    <li>The accident statement, signed by both drivers if one was completed.</li>
    <li>The police report reference, if police attended.</li>
    <li>Your photographs, from both the incident and the original pickup.</li>
    <li>The other driver's details and insurance information.</li>
    <li>Every receipt: recovery, taxi, accommodation, replacement vehicle.</li>
    <li>The final invoice and damage assessment from the rental company.</li>
    <li>Your third-party excess policy number, if you bought one.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Keep all of it until the deposit has been released and any charge on your
    card has settled. Claims on excess policies are usually made after the
    rental company has charged you, not before.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
